title: "Adaptive Mathematical Priors Steered by Expert Disagreement for Tabular Prior-Data Fitted Networks: Theory and a Pre-Registered Evaluation"
abstract: "Prior-data fitted networks (PFNs) learn tabular prediction from synthetic tasks, so their accuracy depends on the prior. We ask whether re-weighting a mathematical prior during pretraining, towards cells where the PFN disagrees with a committee of classical experts, yields a better regressor at equal compute. MAPLE combines a {{ S.n_cells }}-cell mathematical task generator, a KL-regularised adaptive sampler with proofs of non-collapse, stability, finite-sample accuracy and bounded prior distortion, and competence routing between the PFN and the experts. In a pre-registered evaluation on {{ R['128'].n_datasets_full }} regression datasets at three training-set sizes and eight synthetic suites, the adaptive sampler was indistinguishable from a uniform prior (pooled out-of-distribution difference {{ pct(-SY.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood.median_diff) }}, Holm-adjusted p = {{ num(SY.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood.p_holm_family, 2) }}): disagreement scores varied too little across cells to move it. The mathematical prior beat a structural-causal-model prior by {{ pct(S.stats.ablation.P0.synthetic.delta_vs_ref, 0) }} on synthetic suites but not on real data. Routing reduced the PFN's median error by {{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }} to {{ reduction(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }} and, with 128 training rows, outperformed tuned CatBoost (Holm-adjusted p = {{ num(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T'].p_holm_family, 3) }}); with more rows, stacking the experts alone was as accurate.{% if ns.ext_best %} Large pretrained foundation models (TabPFN v2, TabICL), run as external references, had the lowest mean error at every size.{% endif %} At this scale, pretraining compute and prior complexity matter more than adaptive weighting."
keywords: [prior-data fitted networks, tabular regression, synthetic pretraining, adaptive sampling, expert disagreement, distributionally robust optimisation, mathematical priors, out-of-distribution generalisation]
